#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228864 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2e69075-ef65-5a18-b911-601b515a9a84" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying d'Alembert Differential Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "e9cb7978-20da-52f5-be9a-4ce65484a5e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-21716" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a d'Alembert differential equation?\n\n- $yy' = x^2y + e^x$\n\n- $y = x\\ln(1+y') + (y')^2$\n\n- $y + \\dfrac{x}{1+y'} - y' = 0$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny = xf(y') + g(y'),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of $y'.$ \n\nNote that, in the special case where $f(y') = y',$ the equation reduces to a Clairaut differential equation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a d'Alembert differential equation. Solving for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{e^x}{y' - x^2},\n$$\n\nwhich cannot be written in the form $y = xf(y') + g(y').$\n\n- Equation II is a d'Alembert differential equation of the form $y = xf(y') + g(y')$ with\n\n$$\nf(y') = \\ln(1+y'), \\qquad g(y') = (y')^2.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is a d'Alembert differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny = y' - \\dfrac{x}{1+y'},\n$$\n\nwhich is of the form $y = xf(y') + g(y')$ with\n\n$$\nf(y') = -\\dfrac{1}{1+y'}, \\qquad g(y') = y'.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228865 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6bb3f2e-ee56-56f2-94f2-4b454a187ab7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution"}] :question/id #uuid "81f19562-1aee-5d9c-955c-4de09467906f" :question/math-academy-id "e-21717" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny = x\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}-1\\right)^2 + 2\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right).\n$$\n\nIf $y'(x) = t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t), y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\ny(t) = \\left( \\dfrac{2\\ln|t| - t^2+C}{2(t-1)^2} \\right) f(t) + g(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find $f(t)+g(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + \\dfrac{2x}{t-1} = \\frac{1+t}{t(1-t)}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny = xf(y') + g(y'),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of $y'.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution $y'(x) = t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + \\dfrac{2x}{t-1} = \\frac{1+t}{t(1-t)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + P(t)x = Q(t)\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 2/(t-1)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t-1|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =(t-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x & =(t-1)^{2}\\frac{1+t}{t(1-t)} \\\\ (t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x & =\\frac{1-{t}^{2}}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2}) & =\\frac{1-{t}^{2}}{t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\frac{1-{t}^{2}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =\\int \\frac{1}{t}-t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\ x & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-{t}^{2}+C}{2(t-1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\nx(t) = \\dfrac{2\\ln|t| - t^2+C}{2(t-1)^2}.\n$$\n\nTo recover $y$, we substitute $x$ into\n\n$$\ny = x(t-1)^2 + 2t,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-{t}^{2}+C}{2(t-1)^{2}})(t-1)^{2}+2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $f(t)=(t-1)^2,\\ g(t)=2t,$ and\n\n$$\nf(t)+g(t) = t^2 +1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228884 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "126ffd6c-f5a9-564f-935f-398a126d2cbd" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a First-Order Linear Equation From a d'Alembert Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "5aad284d-a5d9-5a90-817b-7786c2e5b062" :question/math-academy-id "e-21773" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny = 2x\\sin\\!\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right) + 5\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2.\n$$\n\nUsing the substitution $t = \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x},$ the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + P(t)x = Q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q.$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny = xf(y') + g(y'),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of $y'.$ \n\nIn our case, we wish to transform the equation\n\n$$\ny = 2x\\sin\\!\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right) + 5\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2\n$$\n\ninto a first-order linear equation for $x = x(t).$ We proceed in three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Substitute, and differentiate with respect to $x.$\n\nWe have the substitution\n\n$$\nt = \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny = 2x\\sin t + 5t^2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+5{t}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum and product rules, and using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{t}^{2}) \\\\ t & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+2x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+10t\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ t & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+10t\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Isolate the derivative and take its reciprocal.\n\nWe isolate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d t}{\\textrm d x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =(2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+10t)\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+10t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d t}{\\textrm d x}=\\dfrac{t-2\\sin t}{2x\\cos t+10t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by taking the reciprocal of each side, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}=\\dfrac{2x\\cos t+10t}{t-2\\sin t}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Express the derivative as a first-order linear equation.\n\nFinally, we write this in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + P(t)x = Q(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+10t}{t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\ (t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+10t \\\\ (t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =10t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}x & =\\frac{10t}{t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t}x & =\\frac{10t}{t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(t) = \\dfrac{2\\cos t}{2\\sin t-t}, \\qquad Q(t) = \\dfrac{10t}{t-2\\sin t}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339029 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2e69075-ef65-5a18-b911-601b515a9a84" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying d'Alembert Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11ddbceb-6b4b-5765-916d-c745dbb9d56c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339031} {:answer/id #uuid "bfc513d0-eec4-5bd7-ac02-278bc05e2776" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339032} {:answer/id #uuid "7b5ee0f6-499e-53b0-9a70-afc0bad17c86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339033} {:answer/id #uuid "f5ea762f-3e61-5622-b6da-c96293f6ff9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339034} {:answer/id #uuid "dd840250-49fa-5551-b384-1fa1c40fec2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339035}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfc513d0-eec4-5bd7-ac02-278bc05e2776" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339032} :answer-field/id #uuid "908e7516-da91-5c7c-9fa1-35a431b67efa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339030}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "103caf7c-e81f-5080-9c10-df7884ea851f" :question/math-academy-id "q-337223" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a d'Alembert differential equation?\n\n- $y=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{′})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+({y}^{′})^{2})$\n- $y{y}^{′}={x}^{3}y+1$\n- $y-x({y}^{′})^{2}+{e}^{{y}^{′}}=0$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nNote that, in the special case where $f({y}^{′})={y}^{′},$ the equation reduces to a Clairaut differential equation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a d'Alembert differential equation of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′})$ with \n\n$$\nf({y}^{′})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{′}),\\,g({y}^{′})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+({y}^{′})^{2}).\n$$\n\n- Equation II is *not* a d'Alembert differential equation. Solving for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}-{x}^{3}},\n$$\n\nwhich cannot be written in the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation III is a d'Alembert differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=x({y}^{′})^{2}-{e}^{{y}^{′}},\n$$\n\nwhich is of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339036 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2e69075-ef65-5a18-b911-601b515a9a84" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying d'Alembert Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0161dc5c-710d-5acf-ae81-da5f1c9e9901" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339038} {:answer/id #uuid "aa6965db-fcda-5bb7-a109-01f94340593d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339039} {:answer/id #uuid "d0c15830-2969-55fa-8ea0-507db94b1743" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339040} {:answer/id #uuid "4502525a-674f-5811-b2b4-178c481b1e2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339041} {:answer/id #uuid "cab1b8fa-53bc-5e22-846f-72b2eb74af69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4502525a-674f-5811-b2b4-178c481b1e2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339041} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ce10c5a-d986-517d-9ca6-37e4445da471" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339037}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ffbf0fc3-7776-519a-bdcb-35221539c005" :question/math-academy-id "q-337226" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a d'Alembert differential equation?\n\n- $y=x({y}^{′})^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{y}^{′})$\n- $y+x({y}^{′})^{2}={2}^{{y}^{′}}$\n- $y{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+x$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nNote that, in the special case where $f({y}^{′})={y}^{′},$ the equation reduces to a Clairaut differential equation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a d'Alembert differential equation of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\nf({y}^{′})=({y}^{′})^{2},\\,g({y}^{′})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{y}^{′}).\n$$\n\n- Equation II is a d'Alembert differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=-x({y}^{′})^{2}+{2}^{{y}^{′}},\n$$\n\nwhich is a d'Alembert differential equation of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′})$ with \n\n$$\nf({y}^{′})=-({y}^{′})^{2},\\,g({y}^{′})={2}^{{y}^{′}}.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a d'Alembert differential equation. Solving for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich cannot be written in the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339043 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2e69075-ef65-5a18-b911-601b515a9a84" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying d'Alembert Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1554d4d-6231-5483-8feb-525585d54593" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339045} {:answer/id #uuid "1d096adf-7023-5a7c-b2d7-a0d1e3708270" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339046} {:answer/id #uuid "3647616c-3000-5179-ab34-0152325ff7dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339047} {:answer/id #uuid "7286b900-9738-50b6-b6a3-e054d4b39ef8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339048} {:answer/id #uuid "42be0dcf-ae61-5dff-95ea-81dd5285b7f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339049}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1554d4d-6231-5483-8feb-525585d54593" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339045} :answer-field/id #uuid "5174e357-d5fd-5d07-9f18-64e60d236a0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "689313e4-b91b-5057-8ee8-cd90bdff6367" :question/math-academy-id "q-337229" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a d'Alembert differential equation?\n\n- $y{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+x$\n- $y+x({y}^{′})^{2}={e}^{{y}^{′}}$\n- $y=x({y}^{′})^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{y}^{′})$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nNote that, in the special case where $f({y}^{′})={y}^{′},$ the equation reduces to a Clairaut differential equation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a d'Alembert differential equation. Solving for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich cannot be written in the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation II is a d'Alembert differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny={e}^{{y}^{′}}-x({y}^{′})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich is of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\nf({y}^{′})=-({y}^{′})^{2},\\,g({y}^{′})={e}^{{y}^{′}}.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is a d'Alembert differential equation of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\nf({y}^{′})=({y}^{′})^{2},\\,g({y}^{′})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{y}^{′}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339050 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2e69075-ef65-5a18-b911-601b515a9a84" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying d'Alembert Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "787848c4-c2a4-5966-b61c-6a87a94fa0c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339052} {:answer/id #uuid "aa26ec4a-cea3-5e34-9982-8541822057ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339053} {:answer/id #uuid "8ebcd1e2-ae77-5c9e-994f-647d591477a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339054} {:answer/id #uuid "9f92af6e-0847-527d-b2ca-16024916e1c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339055} {:answer/id #uuid "d5f49368-dab2-50c2-b5eb-4a88b83f483b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339056}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "787848c4-c2a4-5966-b61c-6a87a94fa0c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339052} :answer-field/id #uuid "140fbda9-3525-5582-a87b-3176bdcb1550" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339051}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "321dd86d-0a40-5d2e-8a10-ba07405bbb7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-337233" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a d'Alembert differential equation?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{3}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{y}$\n- $y+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{y}^{′})={e}^{{y}^{′}}$\n- $y=x({y}^{′})^{3}+\\sqrt{1+({y}^{′})^{2}}$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nNote that, in the special case where $f({y}^{′})={y}^{′},$ the equation reduces to a Clairaut differential equation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a d'Alembert differential equation. Solving for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{y}^{′}-{x}^{3}},\n$$\n\nwhich cannot be written in the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation II is a d'Alembert differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny={e}^{{y}^{′}}-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhich is of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\nf({y}^{′})=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{y}^{′}),\\,g({y}^{′})={e}^{{y}^{′}}.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is a d'Alembert differential equation of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\nf({y}^{′})=({y}^{′})^{3},\\,g({y}^{′})=\\sqrt{1+({y}^{′})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339057 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2e69075-ef65-5a18-b911-601b515a9a84" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying d'Alembert Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80a21573-def3-58cd-b469-647b60ac2071" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339059} {:answer/id #uuid "585bc6ac-a0d3-5674-b2ac-1ecf4c39fcba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339060} {:answer/id #uuid "33e3edb9-eb8e-5609-9d07-7bdbb9111871" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339061} {:answer/id #uuid "13d0a54b-0d01-56a9-af31-dea9a1a2927c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339062} {:answer/id #uuid "4087ae8f-e4c4-52b7-a676-0e5cffa7a713" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339063}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13d0a54b-0d01-56a9-af31-dea9a1a2927c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339062} :answer-field/id #uuid "f62bd3f4-79a5-5edb-b1bb-551afd3c5a34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7bfc3db0-28f4-5f5c-883d-a74f90abd3ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-337245" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a d'Alembert differential equation?\n\n- $y=x({y}^{′})^{3}+({y}^{′})^{2}$\n- $y-x\\sqrt{1+{y}^{′}}+\\frac{1}{1+{y}^{′}}=0$\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{y}$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nNote that, in the special case where $f({y}^{′})={y}^{′},$ the equation reduces to a Clairaut differential equation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a d'Alembert differential equation of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′})$ with \n\n$$\nf({y}^{′})=({y}^{′})^{3},\\,g({y}^{′})=({y}^{′})^{2}.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is a d'Alembert differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=x\\sqrt{1+{y}^{′}}-\\frac{1}{1+{y}^{′}},\n$$\n\nwhich is of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a d'Alembert differential equation. Solving for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich cannot be written in the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339064 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6bb3f2e-ee56-56f2-94f2-4b454a187ab7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "138259d3-557b-5b07-a772-9648d4b47a1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(t)=\\frac{t+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|}{(t-1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339066} {:answer/id #uuid "842f914d-046b-5192-af39-bb4c196c0bd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(t)=\\frac{t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339067} {:answer/id #uuid "81d62d7e-7928-5a76-a3c7-c37f3cd3af57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(t)=\\frac{t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|}{(t-1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339068} {:answer/id #uuid "d1257380-7e6d-5cfc-b3e1-03966eeb658b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(t)=\\frac{t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|}{(t+1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339069} {:answer/id #uuid "88bca819-1653-5ebb-b415-904fd7ebaa4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(t)=\\frac{t-1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|}{(t-1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339070}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81d62d7e-7928-5a76-a3c7-c37f3cd3af57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(t)=\\frac{t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|}{(t-1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339068} :answer-field/id #uuid "a209e160-2816-572c-83d2-a9ea6e94cc56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339065}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2d13645f-14c8-548f-84bd-b0359fdd5dce" :question/math-academy-id "q-337683" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nIf ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t),y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{C}{(t-1)^{2}}+f(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find the function $f(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2}{t-1}x=\\frac{1}{t(t-1)}.\n$$\n\n*You may also wish to make use of the following result (constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int (1-\\frac{1}{t})\\,\\text{d}t=t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2}{t-1}x=\\frac{1}{t(t-1)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t),\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t}={e}^{\\int \\frac{2}{t-1}\\,\\text{d}t}={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t-1|}=(t-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x & =1-\\frac{1}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2}) & =1-\\frac{1}{t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int (1-\\frac{1}{t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|+C \\\\ x & =\\frac{C}{(t-1)^{2}}+\\frac{t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|}{(t-1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{C}{(t-1)^{2}}+\\frac{t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|}{(t-1)^{2}}\n$$\n\nand finally, $f(t)=\\frac{t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|}{(t-1)^{2}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339071 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6bb3f2e-ee56-56f2-94f2-4b454a187ab7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30c20f35-ae3f-5da1-bb0c-f96b6c862d96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(t)=4-3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339073} {:answer/id #uuid "21a0589f-f6fe-56fb-9cbd-6f6facc6e6e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(t)=4-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339074} {:answer/id #uuid "6e33fd0d-e752-50d1-ad81-d31d5112603d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(t)=4+3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339075} {:answer/id #uuid "20c9c7a0-92cf-54b1-ba4c-b1baed0794be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(t)=5-3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339076} {:answer/id #uuid "64734250-da3c-5d28-9449-083df2e2ad4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(t)=3-4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30c20f35-ae3f-5da1-bb0c-f96b6c862d96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(t)=4-3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339073} :answer-field/id #uuid "2752a01b-5638-5477-8e4e-774be2937898" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339072}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e9151b70-2cb0-5312-ba19-ff72c23b0f47" :question/math-academy-id "q-337699" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x(1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+\\frac{3}{2}{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nIf ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t),y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\nx(t)=C{e}^{-t}+f(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find the function $f(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x=1-3t.\n$$\n\n*You may also wish to make use of the following result (constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t=(t-1){e}^{t}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x=1-3t.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t}={e}^{\\int 1\\,\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x{e}^{t} & =(1-3t){e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x{e}^{t}) & =(1-3t){e}^{t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int (1-3t){e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x{e}^{t} & =\\int (1-3t){e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x{e}^{t} & =\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t-3\\int t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x{e}^{t} & ={e}^{t}-3(t-1){e}^{t}+C \\\\ x & =4-3t+C{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\nx(t)=C{e}^{-t}+4-3t\n$$\n\nand finally, $f(t)=4-3t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339078 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6bb3f2e-ee56-56f2-94f2-4b454a187ab7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b555bf5-8563-5912-b8aa-5092cc5c2456" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339080} {:answer/id #uuid "957726a9-5399-5e2d-93c2-57d65d3a747c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}+2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339081} {:answer/id #uuid "96f62f42-15f4-507d-acec-0be367977493" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{t}^{2}-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339082} {:answer/id #uuid "de439b4c-1956-592d-9b4c-7639a4abeca0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339083} {:answer/id #uuid "9cba12c4-5017-5926-9ef7-0c178b5b1715" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{t}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de439b4c-1956-592d-9b4c-7639a4abeca0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339083} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcd69973-65c9-51e4-97b5-0155ee907745" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339079}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0c2b1c78-5509-5e6f-b887-46bb53bd6ea6" :question/math-academy-id "q-337721" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}-2(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}).\n$$\n\nIf ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t),y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\ny(t)=(\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-6t+{t}^{2}+C}{2(t-1)^{2}})f(t)+g(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find $f(t)+g(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1}=\\frac{2-t}{t(1-t)}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1}=\\frac{2-t}{t(1-t)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 2/(t-1)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t-1|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =(t-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x & =(t-1)^{2}\\frac{2-t}{t(1-t)} \\\\ (t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x & =\\frac{(1-t)(2-t)}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2}) & =\\frac{2-t}{t}(1-t) \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\frac{2-t}{t}(1-t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =\\int (\\frac{2}{t}-3+t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-3t+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\ x & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-6t+{t}^{2}+C}{2(t-1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that \n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-6t+{t}^{2}+C}{2(t-1)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo recover $y$, we substitute $x$ into\n\n$$\ny=x{t}^{2}-2t,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =(\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-6t+{t}^{2}+C}{2(t-1)^{2}}){t}^{2}-2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $f(t)={t}^{2},\\text{ }g(t)=-2t,$ and\n\n$$\nf(t)+g(t)={t}^{2}-2t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339085 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6bb3f2e-ee56-56f2-94f2-4b454a187ab7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bcc3e7b4-34b2-52db-b638-548c3e47a758" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}-t-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339087} {:answer/id #uuid "f7b517ad-c08e-5682-b934-617f61c10ff7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{t}^{2}+t-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339088} {:answer/id #uuid "08929cf8-04b6-5dfc-84ef-dea55f47786e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{t}^{2}+t+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339089} {:answer/id #uuid "e973b2d9-41fa-54df-ad5d-697fc7a97fe7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}+t+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339090} {:answer/id #uuid "96c1956a-e826-5c35-896c-a9d87b4af763" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{t}^{2}-t+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08929cf8-04b6-5dfc-84ef-dea55f47786e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{t}^{2}+t+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339089} :answer-field/id #uuid "884136c1-0552-5e75-a8ab-27e04fb189ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339086}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1ce78930-00d8-54c5-aa83-81d95802b525" :question/math-academy-id "q-337727" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2)-{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t),y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\ny(t)=(C{e}^{-t/2}+2t-4)f(t)+g(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find $f(t)+g(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{2}x=t.\n$$\n\n*You may also wish to make use of the following result (constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int t{e}^{t/2}\\,\\text{d}t=(2t-4){e}^{t/2}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{2}x=t.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{t/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t/2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{t/2} & =t{e}^{t/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x{e}^{t/2}) & =t{e}^{t/2} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x{e}^{t/2})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int t{e}^{t/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x{e}^{t/2} & =(2t-4){e}^{t/2}+C \\\\ x & =2t-4+C{e}^{-t/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\nx(t)=C{e}^{-t/2}+2t-4.\n$$\n\nTo recover $y$, we substitute $x$ into\n\n$$\ny=x(t+2)-{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =(C{e}^{-t/2}+2t-4)(t+2)-{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $f(t)=t+2,\\text{ }g(t)=-{t}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\nf(t)+g(t)=-{t}^{2}+t+2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339092 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6bb3f2e-ee56-56f2-94f2-4b454a187ab7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ccbecb1-bab5-51a2-90cd-8f5f108862de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339094} {:answer/id #uuid "dc15a156-b02c-5933-b4fd-6adc6acb8b63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339095} {:answer/id #uuid "eb034691-5957-5401-8d17-3633ef30a615" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339096} {:answer/id #uuid "f509f735-91fe-5b12-baf3-c26186368d5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339097} {:answer/id #uuid "b171348c-29b6-5a10-99b4-ea9e8c853e23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339098}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc15a156-b02c-5933-b4fd-6adc6acb8b63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339095} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bc299b1-4c30-5f28-933e-2028cd25eb8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339093}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4b05fd8b-2a28-5543-b46b-05a4d23444e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-337864" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}-2{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t),y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\ny(t)=(\\frac{2{t}^{2}-4t+C}{(t-1)^{2}})f(t)+g(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find $f(t)+g(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1}=\\frac{4}{t-1}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1}=\\frac{4}{t-1}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 2/(t-1)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t-1|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =(t-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(t-1)^{2}\\frac{2x}{t-1} & =(t-1)^{2}\\frac{4}{t-1} \\\\ (t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x & =4(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2}) & =4(t-1) \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 4(t-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =\\int (4t-4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =2{t}^{2}-4t+C \\\\ x & =\\frac{2{t}^{2}-4t+C}{(t-1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2{t}^{2}-4t+C}{(t-1)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo recover $y$, we substitute $x$ into\n\n$$\ny=x{t}^{2}-2{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =(\\frac{2{t}^{2}-4t+C}{(t-1)^{2}}){t}^{2}-2{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $f(t)={t}^{2},\\text{ }g(t)=-2{t}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\nf(t)+g(t)=-{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339099 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "126ffd6c-f5a9-564f-935f-398a126d2cbd" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a First-Order Linear Equation From a d'Alembert Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d8511f6-b720-522d-9040-efc7a18dc15f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339101} {:answer/id #uuid "12a52341-b0c9-52fd-a097-edecf8b68331" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339102} {:answer/id #uuid "006c8ac8-966c-526e-811b-0349c9de1812" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t(t-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339103} {:answer/id #uuid "48be27cd-e17f-569a-a557-2f25b2fa8d2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339104}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12a52341-b0c9-52fd-a097-edecf8b68331" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339102} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0b321ee-223c-5c04-88d7-787e60bd30e0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339100} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ecafe733-668f-51c0-9547-88b3439786f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t(1-t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339106} {:answer/id #uuid "bb4898a4-5d7f-5025-880e-52b6e27d2254" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t(2-t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339107} {:answer/id #uuid "a11e37b3-4c53-5215-8cf7-fddfcef86c09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339108} {:answer/id #uuid "7726bfda-8fb8-5e8e-9f6c-c000c4d319e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t(1-t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339109}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecafe733-668f-51c0-9547-88b3439786f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t(1-t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339106} :answer-field/id #uuid "2be8351f-4fe0-53c1-9b6d-03664ed23c7f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3c993f1a-3881-5577-a63a-84f119de18f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-365332" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation, defined for $t>0:$\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nUsing the substitution $t=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q.$\n\n$\\,\\,P(t)=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,Q(t)=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nIn our case, we wish to transform the equation\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\ninto a first-order linear equation for $x=x(t).$ We proceed in three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Substitute, and differentiate with respect to $x.$\n\nWe have the substitution\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=x{t}^{2}+t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{t}^{2}+t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum and product rules and using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{t}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(t) \\\\ t & ={t}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({t}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(t) \\\\ t & ={t}^{2}+2xt\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Isolate the derivative and take its reciprocal.\n\nWe isolate $\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-{t}^{2} & =2xt\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ t(1-t) & =(2xt+1)\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{t(1-t)}{2xt+1} & =\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{t(1-t)}{2xt+1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by taking the reciprocal of each side, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2xt+1}{t(1-t)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Express the derivative as a first-order linear equation.\n\nFinally, we write this in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2xt+1}{t(1-t)} \\\\ t(1-t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2xt+1 \\\\ t(1-t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-2xt & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{2xt}{t(1-t)} & =\\frac{1}{t(1-t)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{2x}{1-t} & =\\frac{1}{t(1-t)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1} & =\\frac{1}{t(1-t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{2}{t-1},\\,Q(t)=\\frac{1}{t(1-t)}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339110 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "126ffd6c-f5a9-564f-935f-398a126d2cbd" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a First-Order Linear Equation From a d'Alembert Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d601e62f-ebc7-58e0-aead-1cc4435c308f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339112} {:answer/id #uuid "627c361e-eb2b-5682-88df-0bc010cde681" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-2}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339113} {:answer/id #uuid "bbf017d2-f4ac-5eb7-b7fb-1563031943b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t(t-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339114} {:answer/id #uuid "70c3bb7a-d2de-5ceb-a75c-313f7513639d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d601e62f-ebc7-58e0-aead-1cc4435c308f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339112} :answer-field/id #uuid "24efc0c3-9c16-571a-941e-c4438c2041b5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339111} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad8ec387-74cf-5ba5-a52f-5e95ec87ac3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-1}{t(t-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339117} {:answer/id #uuid "9c08e7b4-6b84-5f60-96d2-368471e33e60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t(t-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339118} {:answer/id #uuid "b8ef3702-cdad-5a57-b5b8-ee2a3a515ca6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t(t-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339119} {:answer/id #uuid "6139c3ff-61e3-54c5-a306-8389f933935b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339120}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c08e7b4-6b84-5f60-96d2-368471e33e60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t(t-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339118} :answer-field/id #uuid "244ca02e-9d4e-53be-a4ea-59cf3f7a4308" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2306e9be-a7c2-54a3-8745-8c6e47c3ddd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-365373" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation, defined for $t>0:$\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nUsing the substitution $t=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q.$\n\n$\\,\\,P(t)=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,Q(t)=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nIn our case, we wish to transform the equation\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\ninto a first-order linear equation for $x=x(t).$ We proceed in three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Substitute, and differentiate with respect to $x.$\n\nWe have the substitution\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=x{t}^{2}-t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{t}^{2}-t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum and product rules and using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{t}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(t) \\\\ t & ={t}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({t}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(t) \\\\ t & ={t}^{2}+2xt\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Isolate the derivative and take its reciprocal.\n\nWe isolate $\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-{t}^{2} & =2xt\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ t(1-t) & =(2xt-1)\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{t(1-t)}{2xt-1} & =\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{t(1-t)}{2xt-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by taking the reciprocal of each side, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2xt-1}{t(1-t)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Express the derivative as a first-order linear equation.\n\nFinally, we write this in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2xt-1}{t(1-t)} \\\\ t(1-t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2xt-1 \\\\ t(1-t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-2xt & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{2xt}{t(1-t)} & =\\frac{-1}{t(1-t)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{2x}{1-t} & =\\frac{-1}{t(1-t)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1} & =\\frac{1}{t(t-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{2}{t-1},\\,Q(t)=\\frac{1}{t(t-1)}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339121 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "126ffd6c-f5a9-564f-935f-398a126d2cbd" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a First-Order Linear Equation From a d'Alembert Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49af3203-42aa-50a9-ab67-dfbbb2341b83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339123} {:answer/id #uuid "85a43c0e-3328-51da-b549-60d9765c67a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339124} {:answer/id #uuid "09e2a8bf-a0d4-513c-88f2-2c1762a15908" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339125} {:answer/id #uuid "db17ec7e-82e3-5d4c-a5e3-00d4944e0790" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}{t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09e2a8bf-a0d4-513c-88f2-2c1762a15908" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339125} :answer-field/id #uuid "8275f8bf-4ef9-591b-9984-460c9a71b9e4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339122} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4caaae79-6647-586e-9904-52a49f8c60d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339128} {:answer/id #uuid "2b95c0ca-ad09-539f-bc7f-7af83d9e8a5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339129} {:answer/id #uuid "4540c63d-ce2a-5f1e-bb52-9d8d80dd9085" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339130} {:answer/id #uuid "3a60d147-ee75-5e9f-a0ae-45c370eb7da3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}{t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339131}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4caaae79-6647-586e-9904-52a49f8c60d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339128} :answer-field/id #uuid "b336962d-d025-5063-89fa-07abe2eb229c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "150152c9-bc0a-5dc6-b37c-9bddd7008264" :question/math-academy-id "q-365376" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=xsin\\,(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the substitution $t=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q.$\n\n$\\,\\,P(t)=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,Q(t)=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nIn our case, we wish to transform the equation\n\n$$\ny=xsin\\,(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\ninto a first-order linear equation for $x=x(t).$ We proceed in three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Substitute, and differentiate with respect to $x.$\n\nWe have the substitution\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum and product rules and using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(t) \\\\ t & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(t) \\\\ t & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Isolate the derivative and take its reciprocal.\n\nWe isolate $\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+1)\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+1} & =\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by taking the reciprocal of each side, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+1}{t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Express the derivative as a first-order linear equation.\n\nFinally, we write this in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+1}{t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)} \\\\ (t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+1 \\\\ (t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}{t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)} & =\\frac{1}{t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-t} & =\\frac{1}{t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-t},\\,Q(t)=\\frac{1}{t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339132 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "126ffd6c-f5a9-564f-935f-398a126d2cbd" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a First-Order Linear Equation From a d'Alembert Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d388650f-b02b-5a36-b8aa-9463592f4e4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339134} {:answer/id #uuid "025329f5-786c-5d12-ac51-c07e1a27b9ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339135} {:answer/id #uuid "9ad4c884-5086-5a77-8955-827336e1edc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t(t-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339136} {:answer/id #uuid "0d985ce9-1500-5408-8bc5-80ad6b4bc1bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339137}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "025329f5-786c-5d12-ac51-c07e1a27b9ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339135} :answer-field/id #uuid "83ceece8-8dac-5280-8c61-005296e37535" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339133} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa389b09-27e7-5d61-8dde-495e97b26b1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t(1-{t}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339139} {:answer/id #uuid "8b6a39b7-1215-5669-960d-385ac94aba02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t(2-{t}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339140} {:answer/id #uuid "0922d9c5-754f-5a1e-8963-b88a6dffecba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t(1-{t}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339141} {:answer/id #uuid "83c1d9d7-076d-543d-9b17-4dc3b0d56544" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t(1-t)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339142}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa389b09-27e7-5d61-8dde-495e97b26b1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t(1-{t}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339139} :answer-field/id #uuid "d44a843e-36fb-5966-bd28-8d0d5ed0efc7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339138}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "83b1f29c-634d-560e-bd30-467a8e32d303" :question/math-academy-id "q-365383" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation, defined for $t>0:$\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+ln\\,(1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})\n$$\n\nUsing the substitution $t=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q.$\n\n$\\,\\,P(t)=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,Q(t)=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nIn our case, we wish to transform the equation\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+ln\\,(1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})\n$$\n\ninto a first-order linear equation for $x=x(t).$ We proceed in three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Substitute, and differentiate with respect to $x.$\n\nWe have the substitution\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=x{t}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+t).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{t}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum and product rules and using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{t}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+t)) \\\\ t & ={t}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({t}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+t)) \\\\ t & ={t}^{2}+2xt\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{1+t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Isolate the derivative and take its reciprocal.\n\nWe isolate $\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-{t}^{2} & =2xt\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{1+t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ t(1-t) & =(2xt+\\frac{1}{1+t})\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{t(1-t)}{2xt+\\frac{1}{1+t}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{t(1-t)}{2xt+\\frac{1}{1+t}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by taking the reciprocal of each side, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2xt+\\frac{1}{1+t}}{t(1-t)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Express the derivative as a first-order linear equation.\n\nFinally, we write this in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2xt+\\frac{1}{1+t}}{t(1-t)} \\\\ t(1-t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2xt+\\frac{1}{1+t} \\\\ t(1-t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-2xt & =\\frac{1}{1+t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{2xt}{t(1-t)} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{1+t}}{t(1-t)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{2x}{1-t} & =\\frac{1}{t(1-t)(1+t)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1} & =\\frac{1}{t(1-{t}^{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{2}{t-1},\\,Q(t)=\\frac{1}{t(1-{t}^{2})}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339143 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "126ffd6c-f5a9-564f-935f-398a126d2cbd" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a First-Order Linear Equation From a d'Alembert Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47129f3a-e474-59c8-99e3-63ba6a314a4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3t}{{t}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339145} {:answer/id #uuid "bf7dc589-6712-5391-bbac-8429ea9bf09f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{{t}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339146} {:answer/id #uuid "ae7e8502-2426-59c9-b59e-7b16aa759857" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3t}{1-{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339147} {:answer/id #uuid "0b23941f-c760-546f-bff2-c9d3ee30cf71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339148}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47129f3a-e474-59c8-99e3-63ba6a314a4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3t}{{t}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339145} :answer-field/id #uuid "63a6bf39-847c-589a-9371-27fe10d244a5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339144} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d9ede98-49b8-5d78-aa8f-b7b27b88cea9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339150} {:answer/id #uuid "5ba30a04-aaf3-564e-8909-dc1cbf42e363" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t(1-{t}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339151} {:answer/id #uuid "bb1dd85f-ca9e-5133-b05a-63b7ca9018c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t}{1-{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339152} {:answer/id #uuid "fb269bb8-0a1e-5f1d-ab58-1818d33a3a69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{1-{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339153}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d9ede98-49b8-5d78-aa8f-b7b27b88cea9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339150} :answer-field/id #uuid "b184db88-2cf7-5efe-8b56-a13f2aac19ff" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339149}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "655d56a0-a0fa-5054-8a82-3c774367f365" :question/math-academy-id "q-365386" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation, defined for $t>0:$\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{3}+ln\\,(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})\n$$\n\nUsing the substitution $t=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q.$\n\n$\\,\\,P(t)=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,Q(t)=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nIn our case, we wish to transform the equation\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{3}+ln\\,(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})\n$$\n\ninto a first-order linear equation for $x=x(t).$ We proceed in three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Substitute, and differentiate with respect to $x.$\n\nWe have the substitution\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=x{t}^{3}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{t}^{3}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum and product rules and using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{t}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t) \\\\ t & ={t}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({t}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t) \\\\ t & ={t}^{3}+3x{t}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Isolate the derivative and take its reciprocal.\n\nWe isolate $\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-{t}^{3} & =3x{t}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ t(1-{t}^{2}) & =(\\frac{3x{t}^{3}+1}{t})\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})}{3x{t}^{3}+1} & =\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})}{3x{t}^{3}+1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by taking the reciprocal of each side, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3x{t}^{3}+1}{{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Express the derivative as a first-order linear equation.\n\nFinally, we write this in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3x{t}^{3}+1}{{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})} \\\\ {t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =3x{t}^{3}+1 \\\\ {t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-3x{t}^{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{3x{t}^{3}}{{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})} & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{3tx}{1-{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{3tx}{{t}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{3t}{{t}^{2}-1},\\,Q(t)=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})}.\n$$"}]}
